  "preamble": "Velar palatalization in Faroese turned the aspirated velar stop [kʰ] into the aspirated palato-alveolar affricate [tʃʰ] before front vowels and /j/. Word-initial Old Norse k before i, e, y and ey is pronounced [tʃʰ] in Faroese; the same process turned unaspirated /k/ into [tʃ]. Faroese phonology still describes /kʰ, k/ as palatalizing to [tʃʰ, tʃ] before /iː, ɪ, eː, ɛ, ɛi, j/.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Affrication, Fronting",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Faroese",
      "languageFamily": "North Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Norse kenna 'to know'",
          "to": "kenna /ˈt͡ʃʰɛnːa/",
          "note": "Initial k before the front vowel e became the aspirated affricate."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Norse kirkja 'church'",
          "to": "kirkja /ˈt͡ʃʰɪr̥t͡ʃa/",
          "note": "Initial k before the front vowel i gave [tʃʰ]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Norse kyssa 'to kiss'",
          "to": "kyssa /ˈt͡ʃʰɪsːa/",
          "note": "The front rounded vowel y also triggered the change."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
